{
  // The Laya decision layer (packages/core/src/decisions, schema config/schemas/decisions.ts).
  //
  // Laya is a small local choice model in the `laya` sidecar. It answers the swarm's yes/no and
  // pick-one questions in milliseconds, where the incumbent is an LLM call of one to two seconds
  // on the shared GPU. The incumbent is the LLM call or rule that decides the point today. It
  // stays the fallback, and it is the teacher Laya is measured, and fine-tuned, against.
  //
  // The sidecar's address comes from SAI_LAYA_URL, which `sai start --laya` sets. Without the
  // sidecar every point is decided by its incumbent, whatever the modes below say.
  //
  // "adaptive": the incumbent decides until the decision ledger (.starlingai/decisions/ledger.jsonl)
  // shows that Laya agrees with it, per point, language and answer, at a confidence where the
  // agreement's lower bound reaches targetAgreement over at least minSamples cases. From then on
  // Laya decides those cases, the incumbent is not waited for, and auditRate of them still goes
  // to the incumbent so the agreement keeps being measured.
  //
  // The stock checkpoint is not expected to qualify for much before it is fine-tuned on the ledger,
  // so this starts out as shadow mode: nothing changes until the evidence says it may.
  //
  // To only watch: set "defaultMode": "shadow". To switch one point off: "points": { "<id>": { "mode": "off" } }.
  // Points: fast_lane, source_sensitive, ungrounded_draft, slices_disagree, goal_met,
  // finding_relevant (shared-fact distillation: Laya may only say "nothing relevant", which saves
  // the extraction call) and run_drifting (the semantic progress judge, itself off by default:
  // Laya may only say "on track"; winding a run down stays the routing tier's call).
  // Report: `pnpm --filter @starlingai/core decisions:report`.
  //
  // "browser": laya-browser beside the browser agent. It clicks and selects on its own — never a
  // form submit, a download, a new tab, an internal host, typing or finishing — and the agent's
  // model takes every other step. Its steps run as the agent's own browser_click /
  // browser_select_option calls (approvals and guards apply) and are marked as its in the call.
  // Both choices of every step go to the audit (browser_step) and to
  // .starlingai/decisions/browser-ledger.jsonl, with the page as it was read.
  //
  // "adaptive" acts where the comparisons with the model show it agrees at its confidence, with
  // the settings above. "drive" acts above a fixed driveMinProbability instead — but its
  // probabilities are not calibrated: on real pages its right and its wrong elements both came at
  // 0.6–0.7, so 0.9 never acts and 0.6 acts wrongly about half the time. "shadow" only compares.
  //
  // Fine-tuning on the swarm's own cases, once the ledgers have grown (a few hundred each):
  //   pnpm --filter @starlingai/core decisions:export
  //   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.gpu.yml --profile laya run --rm laya python -m app.train decision
  //   (and: ... python -m app.train browser), then restart the laya service.
  // A run replaces the served checkpoint only when it agrees with the incumbent more often on
  // held-out cases (.starlingai/laya/<model>/runs/<run>/metrics.json says why). The gate's evidence
  // is kept per checkpoint version, so a new one earns its handover from its own cases.
  "decisions": {
    "defaultMode": "adaptive",
    "adaptive": { "targetAgreement": 0.9, "minSamples": 30, "auditRate": 0.1 },
    "browser": { "mode": "adaptive" }
  }
}
